with q as (
  select $astroKB_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Gerard Kuiper predicted a belt of icy bodies beyond Neptune in 1951, yet the region named for him ended up being an ironic tribute. What did Kuiper get wrong, and why?", "options": ["He predicted the belt would form but also argued it no longer existed in the present solar system, because he thought Pluto was roughly Earth-sized and massive enough to have gravitationally cleared and scattered the region away; in fact Pluto is smaller than the Moon and far too feeble to sweep anything, so the belt he thought dispersed was still sitting there.", "He placed the belt at the wrong distance, predicting bodies at 40 AU when the classical belt actually lies between 42 and 48 AU, so his prediction had to be corrected by later observers.", "He argued the belt was made of rocky asteroids rather than icy bodies, an error corrected only when 1992 QB1 was found to be red and ice-rich.", "He believed the belt had been swept up by Neptune's migration, whereas it was actually cleared by a passing star in the Sun's birth cluster."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Kuiper was right about the formation of an icy belt in the outer disc but wrong about its survival: he believed Pluto was roughly Earth-sized and massive enough to have gravitationally cleared the region, scattering the belt away so that it no longer existed. He was wrong because he was wrong about Pluto's mass, which turned out to be smaller than our own Moon, far too feeble to sweep anything. The belt he thought had been dispersed was still there. This is the tidy irony noted in the chapter: the region is named for a man who predicted it and then argued it had been swept empty. The other options misstate his error: the distance figures and the ice composition were not his mistakes, and he invoked Pluto's clearing, not stellar or migration-driven sweeping."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The classical belt contains two overlapping populations, 'hot' and 'cold' (referring to dynamical, not thermal, temperature). What is the standard reading of why they differ, and what single feature most strongly certifies that the cold population has been left undisturbed?", "options": ["Both populations formed in place beyond 42 AU, and the hot objects were later heated by radiation, which reddened them unevenly; the uniform red colour of the cold group certifies their calm.", "The cold objects formed in situ beyond about 42 AU and were never seriously disturbed, while the hot objects formed closer to the Sun and were flung outward and stirred to high inclinations during the giant planets' migration; the survival of fragile, wide, loosely-bound binaries, which a violent collision-rich history would break apart, is the linchpin certifying the cold belt was left alone.", "The two populations formed at the same location but the hot one was captured into Neptune's resonances, which pumped up its inclinations; the plutino overlap certifies the cold belt's calm.", "The cold objects are simply the small end of a single population and the hot objects the large end; the size difference certifies nothing about disturbance, only about accretion time."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The now widely accepted reading is that the two populations did not form in the same place. The cold objects formed in situ beyond about 42 AU and were never seriously disturbed, which is why their orbits are pristine (low eccentricity and, above all, low inclination, within a few degrees of the plane), why their uniform red colour reflects a single formation environment, and why their fragile binaries survive. The hot objects formed closer to the Sun in the denser inner disc and were flung outward and stirred up during the giant planets' migration, arriving with a range of colours and inclinations reaching twenty or thirty degrees and beyond. The linchpin of the argument is the wide, loosely-bound binaries: perhaps a quarter to a third of the cold population are such pairs, and a wide loosely-bound pair cannot survive a violent collision-rich history, so their mere existence certifies the cold belt has been left alone. The chapter flags that this same physics of gentleness returns with Arrokoth."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Using Kepler's third law (P^2 = a^3, P in years, a in AU) and Neptune's period of 164.8 years, where do the 3:2 (plutino) and 2:1 (twotino) resonances sit, and what does the heavy crowding of the plutinos record?", "options": ["The plutinos sit at about 47.7 AU and the twotinos at about 39.4 AU; the crowding records the primordial density peak of the solar nebula at those radii.", "Both resonances sit near 43 AU, the location of the cold classical belt; the crowding records objects that formed in place and never moved.", "The plutinos sit at about 39.4 AU (a body completing two orbits for every three of Neptune's, so P = 247.2 years) and the twotinos at about 47.7 AU (P = 329.6 years); the heavy crowding of the plutinos is a direct fossil of Neptune's outward migration, whose resonances swept outward ahead of it like a snowplough and captured bodies in their path.", "The plutinos sit at about 39.4 AU and the twotinos at about 55 AU; the crowding records a collision that shattered a larger body at Pluto's distance."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "A 3:2 resonant body completes two orbits for every three of Neptune's, so its period is P = (3/2) x 164.8 = 247.2 years, giving a = 247.2^(2/3) approximately 39.4 AU, essentially Pluto's semi-major axis, which is why the members are called plutinos. The 2:1 gives P = 2 x 164.8 = 329.6 years and a = 329.6^(2/3) approximately 47.7 AU at the outer edge of the classical belt, home to the thinner twotino population. The crowding is not a density peak or a collision remnant: it is a direct fossil of Neptune's outward migration. As Neptune migrated its resonances swept outward ahead of it like a snowplough, capturing bodies in their path and dragging them along while pumping up their eccentricities. You cannot fill a resonance to that degree with a stationary planet, so the overpopulation is a recording of the planet moving."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Arrokoth is described as the strongest single piece of direct evidence for how planetesimals form. Precisely which observed features make it evidence for gentle pebble-cloud collapse (streaming instability) rather than violent hierarchical collisional accretion?", "options": ["Its two lobes are undeformed and joined at a smooth neck with no crater and no sign of impact heating, and they share identical composition, colour, and aligned orientations; this implies a merger at no more than the few-metres-per-second mutual escape velocity of two co-formed, gravitationally-bound bodies, whereas collisional accretion would bring lobes from unrelated orbits at hundreds of metres to kilometres per second, deforming, cratering, and heating them.", "Its reddish colour and methanol ice prove it condensed directly from the solar nebula without ever passing through a pebble stage.", "Its 36-kilometre size is exactly the size that the metre-size barrier predicts for the largest body that can form by sticking, ruling out collapse.", "Its highly elongated shape shows it was spun up by repeated grazing impacts, which only violent accretion can produce, so it actually supports the collisional picture."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The whole point is the morphology. Arrokoth is a contact binary of two flattened lobes (about 21 and 15 km) joined at a narrow, smooth neck with no crater, no compression fracturing, no deformation, and no sign of impact heating, and the lobes share identical composition and colour (the same red, the same ices including methanol) with aligned poles. For two bodies to touch and stay touching without deforming they must meet at no more than about their mutual escape velocity, which for Arrokoth's size and comet-like density of a few hundred kg/m3 is only a few metres per second, roughly walking pace. Hierarchical collisional accretion would bring two lobes from unrelated orbits at random relative velocities of hundreds of metres to kilometres per second, an impact that deforms, shatters, craters, and heats. A collapsing pebble cloud with a little net rotation instead fragments into two bound, co-formed, similarly-oriented bodies that spiral together gently and touch at or below escape velocity by construction. The observed morphology is what gentle cloud collapse predicts and what violent accretion forbids."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Sedna (perihelion about 76 AU, aphelion about 936 AU) defines the 'detached' population. Why is its orbit a genuine problem, and what distinguishes detached objects from scattered-disc objects like Eris?", "options": ["Sedna's orbit is problematic because it is nearly circular, unlike the eccentric scattered disc; detached objects differ from scattered ones by having low inclinations.", "Sedna's 76 AU perihelion is more than double Neptune's 30 AU distance, so Neptune, which requires a close encounter to scatter a body, can never have reached it; detached objects have perihelia lifted clear of Neptune (decoupled), whereas scattered-disc objects keep perihelia near Neptune (around 30 to 38 AU) so Neptune can still reach and re-scatter them.", "Sedna is problematic because it is more massive than Pluto yet lies beyond the classical belt; detached objects differ from scattered ones only in being larger.", "Sedna's orbit is problematic because it lies in the 2:1 resonance with Neptune; detached objects differ from scattered ones by being resonant rather than eccentric."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The number that matters is Sedna's 76 AU perihelion, more than double Neptune's distance of about 30 AU. To gravitationally scatter a body outward Neptune must have a close encounter with it, which requires the body's perihelion to dip down near Neptune's orbit; Sedna's perihelion has never come anywhere near, so whatever emplaced Sedna, it was not Neptune, and Neptune is the outermost thing we know to be there. That is the defining feature of the detached population: perihelia lifted clear of the giant planets, orbits decoupled from the known solar system. Scattered-disc objects like Eris (perihelion near 38 AU) instead keep their perihelia coupled to Neptune, still coming close enough at nearest approach to be scattered again, a population being actively stirred rather than left in peace. Detaching an object requires lifting its perihelion after it was scattered, by a passing star in the birth cluster, an ejected rogue planet, or a distant still-present perturber."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Lay out the full argument, step by step, for why a single six-hour flyby of the 36-kilometre body Arrokoth is claimed to be the strongest direct evidence that planetesimals form by pebble-cloud collapse (the streaming instability) rather than by hierarchical collisional accretion. Be explicit about what the two barriers to collisional growth are, what streaming instability does instead, and which specific observed feature of Arrokoth is load-bearing.", "rubric": "A strong answer establishes the two theories and the decisive observation. (1) The traditional theory is hierarchical collisional accretion: dust to pebbles to boulders to kilometre bodies by two-body sticking collisions. It stalls at two barriers near one metre: the bouncing/fragmentation barrier (metre-scale bodies collide too fast and too weakly to stick, so they shatter or bounce and growth reverses), and the drift barrier (a metre body feels a headwind because the pressure-supported gas orbits slightly slower, so it loses energy and spirals into the Sun in as little as centuries). (2) The streaming instability skips these: pebbles perturb the gas, which back-reacts, causing them to clump into dense filaments; where a clump's self-gravity takes over it collapses directly, a diffuse swarm perhaps 1000 km across contracting in one gravitational step to a bound body tens to a couple hundred km across. The metre size is never occupied by a solitary body, so neither barrier is crossed. (3) Such a collapsing cloud with net rotation tends to fragment into two comparable co-formed bodies, gravitationally bound from birth, whose mutual orbit shrinks by gas drag until they touch at no more than their mutual escape velocity (a few m/s, walking pace, for Arrokoth's comet-like density of a few hundred kg/m3). (4) The load-bearing observation is Arrokoth's morphology: two undeformed lobes (about 21 and 15 km) joined at a smooth neck with NO crater, NO compression fracturing, NO impact heating, plus identical composition/colour (red, methanol ice) and aligned poles. The absence of deformation and heating bounds the closing speed to a few m/s = the escape velocity. (5) The contrast: collisional accretion would deliver two lobes from unrelated orbits at hundreds of m/s to km/s, which deforms, craters, shatters, and heats, forbidding the observed pristine join; cloud collapse requires the gentle bound merger that is observed. A top answer also notes the corroborating witnesses: the abundant fragile wide near-equal-mass cold-classical binaries with correlated orientations, the deficit of the smallest bodies in the cold belt's size distribution, and the fragile low-density layered comet nuclei, plus that Arrokoth's cold classical location is precisely the quiet place able to preserve a primordial planetesimal for 4.5 billion years."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter presents Planet Nine as a hypothesis that must be weighed rather than repeated, with three parts: a genuine motivation, a serious objection, and no detection. Reconstruct that honest treatment. What is observed and why does it suggest a distant planet; what is the selection-effect objection and why does the word 'characterized' matter (cite OSSOS); and what is the state of the search?", "rubric": "A strong answer treats all three legs honestly. MOTIVATION: In 2016 Batygin and Brown noted that the most extreme trans-Neptunian objects (very large semi-major axes beyond roughly 250 AU, perihelia detached from Neptune) did not appear randomly oriented; their perihelion directions point preferentially the same way and their orbital planes seem tilted in a correlated fashion. Because such orbits are otherwise dynamically independent, a shared alignment demands a common cause, and a single distant massive planet, several Earth masses (estimates ranging from about ten down to perhaps five or six) on a large eccentric inclined orbit of many hundreds of AU, is a physically economical one that would shepherd them into just that clustered configuration via long-term resonant and secular interactions. This is quantitative and falsifiable, predicting further aligned, anti-aligned, and highly inclined objects. OBJECTION (selection effect): the clustering rests on only about a dozen objects found not by one uniform survey but by many heterogeneous searches pointed at different parts of the sky, in different seasons, to different depths. Surveys look where the sky is dark and the target is up, and eccentric objects are only bright near perihelion, so the directions in which we have LOOKED are themselves clustered, which can imprint an apparent alignment even if the true population is uniform. 'Characterized' matters because only a survey that quantified exactly where, how deeply, and with what efficiency it searched can compare its detections against what a given true population would yield through that specific bias, distinguishing 'the objects are clustered' from 'our looking was clustered.' OSSOS did this and found its sample consistent with NO intrinsic clustering; with about a dozen objects the statistics are fragile either way. NO DETECTION: a decade of dedicated searching (infrared surveys ruling out a Saturn-mass body to great distances; optical searches across Pan-STARRS, the Dark Energy Survey, and others) has found nothing at the predicted brightness. Absence of detection is not disproof, because a several-Earth-mass planet at hundreds of AU would be extraordinarily faint and could hide in an unsearched patch, but the hypothesis is still waiting on its evidence. VERDICT: a legitimate, falsifiable hypothesis, motivated by a real pattern, weakened by a real unresolved worry that the pattern is partly an artifact of pointing, and unconfirmed by any sighting; the Vera C. Rubin Observatory, with uniform deep well-characterized all-sky coverage, will settle it two ways at once, statistically (finding the extreme objects in numbers with understood biases) and possibly directly (seeing the planet if it exists)."}]$astroKB_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/small-bodies/kuiper-belt', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
